What Directory Sync can and cannot do here

A sync link is a one-way pair of directory connections, not of servers. Because East and West each expose c=us and c=admin as separate connections, you need one link per branch per direction. One-way East to West is two links. Full bidirectional is four links, and the docs warn that reverse links between the same pair need care (docs/directory-sync-operations.md §11).

Two things I verified in the engine matter for IVIA:

secDN will be dropped from mirrored secUser entries unless you override reference attributes. The default reference-attribute list includes secDN, and the resolver in RecomputeEngine.resolverFor only looks up target DNs in the membership index of the same link. A secUser in the c=admin link points at a demographic entry tracked by the c=us link, so the lookup fails and the value is silently removed from the projection. Set the c=admin sets' reference attributes explicitly to member,uniqueMember,owner so secDN passes through verbatim. That is correct for a mirror because the DN is identical on both sides.
Passwords do not mirror by default. The default excluded list drops userPassword, plus the operational password-policy attributes. If West must accept the same credentials, set an explicit excluded list that omits userPassword, allow pre-encoded passwords in the OUD password policy on West, and give the bind account the privilege to write them. Otherwise expect CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION — pre-encoded passwords are not allowed rows.
Configuration steps (East to West)
Entitle the feature. Set LDAPPORTAL_ENTITLEMENTS_GRANT=DIRECTORY_SYNC on the backend, or carry it in the license. Confirm with the "Granting entitlement(s)" startup log line.
Prepare OUD. Changelog capture reads the external changelog at cn=changelog, which exists on OUD only when replication is enabled on that instance. Your three-node clusters almost certainly already have it. Grant the bind DN used by the East c=us and c=admin connections read access to cn=changelog and to the root DSE attributes firstChangeNumber and lastChangeNumber. The poller reads both links' cursors from the same changelog and filters by set scope, so that is fine.
Pre-create the container skeleton on West. Use applicability filters that select leaf objects only. Missing parent OUs would fail the ADD and sit as FAILED until the reconcile retries, so create ou=people,c=us, secAuthority=Default,c=admin, and the IVIA containers under it first.
Create the links under Superadmin, then Directory Sync:
Field	Link 1	Link 2
Source	East c=us	East c=admin
Target	West c=us	West c=admin
Capture mode	CHANGELOG	CHANGELOG
Changelog format	DSEE_CHANGELOG	DSEE_CHANGELOG
Changelog base DN	cn=changelog	cn=changelog
Create one set per link. Use your real IVIA DNs where I have guessed.
Field	Demographic set	secAuthority set
Identity key	entryUUID	entryUUID
Source scope base DN	c=us (or ou=people,c=us)	secAuthority=Default,c=admin
Scope	SUB	SUB
Applicability filter	(objectClass=inetOrgPerson)	(|(objectClass=secUser)(objectClass=secGroup)(objectClass=secPolicyData))
Target base DN	same as source scope	same as source scope
Reference attributes	leave default	member,uniqueMember,owner
Source anchor attribute	leave blank	leave blank
Delete policy	REVIEW	REVIEW
Reconcile cadence	3600	3600

Leave the anchor blank for a same-DN mirror. Without an anchor the engine's ADD falls through to MODIFY on ENTRY_ALREADY_EXISTS, so pre-existing West entries converge in place. With an anchor configured, every occupied DN that lacks the anchor is quarantined for REVIEW, which for a full mirror means every entry.

Enable and watch. The first reconcile does the bulk load. Use the link and set health rollups, then the inventory, to clear FAILED rows, which will mostly be schema or ACL problems on West. Switch delete policy to DELETE only once a reconcile cycle is clean.






Here are the steps again for EKS. They follow the repo's EKS runbook (docs/deployment-eks-gitlab-ecr.md) with two changes: you reuse your existing secrets instead of generating new ones, and you load your data into RDS before the backend starts for the first time.

Why the order matters

On first start the backend runs Flyway against an empty database and creates the bootstrap superadmin. If you restore your dump after that, it clashes with the tables and rows the app just created. Load your data into RDS first, then deploy (runbook §7).

1. Follow the runbook up to §6, with these changes

§3 RDS: choose PostgreSQL 16.x, since local runs 16.13. The database ldapportal and master user ldapportal the runbook creates are exactly what you need, and the database starts empty. The security group has to allow port 5432 from the EKS nodes.

§4 Secrets Manager: don't generate a new ENCRYPTION_KEY as the runbook shows. Store your current one, otherwise none of the stored directory bind passwords and other secrets can be decrypted. Do the same for JWT_SECRET if you want existing sessions to keep working.

# from the machine with your current .env; tr strips CR/LF (see the runbook's CRLF warning)
aws secretsmanager create-secret --name ldapportal/encryption-key \
  --secret-string "$(grep '^ENCRYPTION_KEY=' .env | cut -d= -f2- | tr -d '\r\n')" --region $REGION
aws secretsmanager create-secret --name ldapportal/jwt-secret \
  --secret-string "$(grep '^JWT_SECRET=' .env | cut -d= -f2- | tr -d '\r\n')" --region $REGION
# db-password = the RDS master password, as in the runbook.
# bootstrap-superadmin-password is still required, but unused: your restored superadmin already exists.

§5–6 images and overlay: keep the community-plus-isva image, which the runbook already uses and which matches the local compose default. Set DB_URL in configmap-patch.yaml to your RDS endpoint with ?sslmode=require.

2. Dump the local database
docker compose stop app      # stop the local app so nothing writes during the dump
docker exec ldap-portal-db pg_dump -U ldapportal -d ldapportal -Fc -f /tmp/ldapportal.dump
docker cp ldap-portal-db:/tmp/ldapportal.dump ./ldapportal.dump

From here on, treat the local instance as read-only. Anything changed in it after the dump won't be migrated.

3. Restore into RDS from inside the cluster

RDS is private, but your EKS nodes can reach it. Run a temporary Postgres pod in the cluster and restore from there:

kubectl apply -f deploy/aws/eks/namespace.yaml

kubectl -n ldapportal run pg-restore --image=postgres:16.13-alpine --restart=Never \
  --command -- sleep 3600
kubectl -n ldapportal wait --for=condition=Ready pod/pg-restore
kubectl -n ldapportal cp ./ldapportal.dump pg-restore:/tmp/ldapportal.dump

kubectl -n ldapportal exec -it pg-restore -- sh -c \
  'PGPASSWORD="<RDS_MASTER_PASSWORD>" pg_restore -h <RDS_ENDPOINT> -U ldapportal -d ldapportal \
     --no-owner --no-acl --exit-on-error /tmp/ldapportal.dump'

# quick sanity check: the Flyway history should list your applied migrations
kubectl -n ldapportal exec -it pg-restore -- sh -c \
  'PGPASSWORD="<RDS_MASTER_PASSWORD>" psql "host=<RDS_ENDPOINT> user=ldapportal dbname=ldapportal sslmode=require" \
     -c "select count(*), max(installed_on) from flyway_schema_history"'

kubectl -n ldapportal delete pod pg-restore
4. Deploy (runbook §7)
kubectl apply -k deploy/aws/eks-overlays/prod
kubectl -n ldapportal rollout status deploy/ldapportal-backend
kubectl -n ldapportal logs deploy/ldapportal-backend | grep -i flyway

Flyway should report that the schema is up to date. If the image is newer than your local build, it will apply only the new migrations. A validation failure almost always means the edition doesn't match.

5. Check it worked
Login: after DNS (runbook §8), sign in with your existing admin and superadmin credentials. The runbook's "log in with BOOTSTRAP_SUPERADMIN_PASSWORD" step doesn't apply, because your restored superadmin already exists.
Encryption key: open a directory and click Test connection. A success confirms ENCRYPTION_KEY is correct.
Row counts: compare a few counts (admins, audit events) against the local database.
Sessions: browser sessions from the local instance won't carry over, because the hostname changes and the cookie is tied to it. Everyone logs in again.
If you already deployed to EKS before restoring
Stop the backend:
kubectl -n ldapportal scale deploy/ldapportal-backend --replicas=0

The HPA doesn't scale up a deployment that's at 0 replicas.

Reset the database from the pg-restore pod with DROP SCHEMA public CASCADE; CREATE SCHEMA public;.
Do the restore in step 3.
Bring the backend back:
kubectl -n ldapportal scale deploy/ldapportal-backend --replicas=2
Afterwards
Keep the local pgdata volume and ldapportal.dump until you're confident in the new setup. Delete the dump file once you're done, because it holds encrypted secrets and audit history.
Turn on RDS automated backups (7 days or more) and deletion protection.
Run make export-config with BASE_URL=https://<HOSTNAME> to get a readable config snapshot of the new install.
